heta }_{R}=0.7297.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/92b256db68d09a07e5072d12cb18470e2c165b6f3c8aa29c6953d9aebb3b024e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.7297 \\\\  & ≈3.8713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & =3.871\\,3-2\\pi ≈-2.4119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈-2.4,\\,x≈-0.7.\n$$" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265339 13194139533913 true] [17592186265338 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533913 true] [17592186265338 1041 "field-1" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265338 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265339 13194139533913 true] [17592186265338 1110 #uuid "e8f8375f-b8b3-5341-a2da-1051cdb5b7b5" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265339 1034 "-2.4" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265339 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533913 true] [17592186265339 1111 #uuid "c46f2a38-ffd0-5e84-a14b-60b184402e1b" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265341 13194139533913 true] [17592186265340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533913 true] [17592186265340 1041 "field-2" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265341 13194139533913 true] [17592186265340 1110 #uuid "7dd25aa3-1391-563d-be1c-84498d724cf4" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265341 1034 "-0.7" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533913 true] [17592186265341 1111 #uuid "6740a829-3fa0-536f-a658-fabe50839ef2" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265342 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265343 13194139533913 true] [17592186265342 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265345 13194139533913 true] [17592186265342 1089 "q-328393" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265342 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533913 true] [17592186265342 1100 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533913 true] [17592186265342 1113 #uuid "a5954b94-b8cd-5696-a81c-2e44aa692bad" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265342 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arcsin\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951962/assets/d50a4a9f62be8b8af4cc657a5b0ab1547288b844eaa05af1a57ed39b8afa0d6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265344 13194139533913 true] [17592186265343 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533913 true] [17592186265343 1041 "field-1" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265343 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265344 13194139533913 true] [17592186265343 1110 #uuid "d4ecfce4-fc0f-5d9f-a177-795e12cbc771" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265344 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533913 true] [17592186265344 1111 #uuid "782b9fca-6fa3-5796-b1a9-015ed90a1631" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265346 13194139533913 true] [17592186265345 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533913 true] [17592186265345 1041 "field-2" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265345 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265346 13194139533913 true] [17592186265345 1110 #uuid "c043081f-89f3-59aa-aa57-36bb165b0966" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265346 1034 "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533913 true] [17592186265346 1111 #uuid "04221237-988a-510a-b6b7-e472f70baacd" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265347 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265348 13194139533913 true] [17592186265347 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265351 13194139533913 true] [17592186265347 1089 "q-330397" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265347 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533913 true] [17592186265347 1100 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533913 true] [17592186265347 1113 #uuid "892abfcf-428b-5897-9666-b647328728e3" 13194139533913 true] [17592186265347 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\